=== task: mcq easy
title: What "amortized" means
prompt: Append on a dynamic array is "O(1) amortized". What does that mean?
* Over many appends, the average cost per append is O(1), even though a few appends cost O(n) :: Right. The rare expensive copies are spread over all the cheap appends.
- Every single append takes O(1) :: The appends that make the array grow copy every item: O(n).
- Appends are O(1) only for small arrays :: The average stays constant for any size.
- Appends are O(1) only if you never append twice :: The more you append, the more the copies average out.
hint: Some appends copy the whole array. How often?
hint: Total work divided by the number of appends.
right: n appends cost under 3n steps in total, so the average is under 3 per append: O(1) amortized, O(n) in the worst single case.
wrong: Amortized means averaged over many operations: the occasional O(n) copy is paid for by all the O(1) appends.

